From 68bf605bbeca36159e42bd003a8fda7b62cabfb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:25:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: remove the temporary stall diagnostics (0031, 0032)

Removal was planned at landing for both, per the hub convention that a
temporary diagnostic patch must not become a permanent tax. This is that
removal, and it is the shipping configuration.

What they bought, and why they can now go:

0031 established that the mid-run stalls are NOT where six earlier
hypotheses put them. pump_ms measured 0.045ms and tail_ms 4.2ms on stall
frames -- indistinguishable from normal frames -- which excluded the
audio ring refill and the game-logic tail outright, and located the cost
entirely inside the render/flip span. It also showed the startup stall
and the mid-run stall share a signature (render_flip carrying ~100% of
the work), correcting an earlier conclusion that they were different
bugs.

0032 split that span further to name the sub-stage.

Everything they measured is recorded in docs/PLAN.md's measured-constants
table, so removing the instrumentation costs no knowledge.

Keeping them would cost real fps: this port has measured diagnostic
overhead twice now -- 0.70ms/frame for SDL/0136's per-yield timing, and
~0.4-0.5fps for the per-frame logging patch 0028 removed. The conditional
emit here is far lighter, but a shipping build should carry none of it.

The frame pacer itself (patch 0030) is untouched. Real-hardware result
stands at 14.94fps, up from 13.992, with steady-state 14.9989fps against
the 15.000fps ceiling lockedFrameRate=15 imposes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
